</span></h2><p style=3D"display:block;color:#333;font:400 18.5px/26px georg=
ia,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>By Amanda Taub</span></p><p style=3D"font:n=
ormal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>France may =
seem like a nation of perpetual protest. But if you are trying to make sens=
e of the latest wave of demonstrations spurred on by young people angry ove=
r dilapidated schools, it might help to cast your mind back just a few year=
s.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;mar=
gin:0 0 15px"><span>Not long ago, the French </span><a href=3D"https://nl.n=
ytimes.com/f/newsletter/hjkMSQL0NqOVGjJUIE-BYg~~/AAAAARA~/gaamUpRFLPrsP7lRd=
DunQw-pYrkEK6l1OVWKmsgcZY4NokxZNWI1MfRcxiivi6976sc3m9VkNfm1yLME4C2d-uQ0LMTV=
EyxQQD2dKStYKPHJ-pfqWcqitSQ20lHUYdbas-7yPRmZ3R8bwxyJ1nxpDXGXzuEkbQtTRoG_cCd=
5U7n_N-3JA9VX1nn_42YtwOFnWTC9pPOo5kJucG-6BboVknlOnti3dDVSDfxAPSlaiq5g55ldIr=
6VmnJbhSofiZ0TbnTXoEJF3SJv2_s1VuduieFfpDHskl3D1eJMLirSNrAnYTALxjNxEDgGjt0Uf=
EcwbDEfKFQ-8dGFQuDQ0RChTIlDiATEGosiYuWWZgGQ_m5J7Ay0KLOXrrT-L3bpE3ZGTgb7akMr=
wih344NfdWcQJw~~" class=3D"css-sdwaa1" style=3D"color:#286ed0;-webkit-text-=
decoration:underline;text-decoration:underline">took to the streets</a><spa=
n> when President Emmanuel Macron raised the retirement age from 62 to 64. =
The outcry was so intense that the government eventually suspended the meas=
ure; France=E2=80=99s pensions remain one of the biggest drains on the budg=
et of a nation with one of the highest debt levels in Europe.</span></p><p =
style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><s=
pan>Today=E2=80=99s protesters have taken to the barricades to demand more =
resources for the young, while the earlier ones marched to protect resource=
s for the old. In both cases, the protesters saw themselves in a fight with=
 the French government; it might be equally valid to say they were =E2=80=
=94 and still are =E2=80=94 in a fight with each other.</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>T=
he dissatisfaction currently roiling Europe, from France to Spain to Britai=
n to Belgium, is about many things: schools, housing, pensions, unemploymen=
t. But step back, and it=E2=80=99s also about one big thing =E2=80=94 a gen=
erational fight over an economic pie fixed in its size by the continent=E2=
=80=99s chronic slow economic growth.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17p=
x/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight=
:700">=E2=80=98Gerontonomia=E2=80=99</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px=
/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>As Europe=E2=80=99s p=
opulation ages, conflict may be unavoidable. Because ultimately, different =
generations have different economic interests, said Tim Vlandas, a professo=
r at Oxford.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;colo=
r:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Young people need investments in education, a=
 strong labor market and affordable housing. People over 65, by contrast, t=
end to want generous pensions and social care, protection for the value of =
their homes and other assets and low inflation, even at the cost of higher =
unemployment. (No one likes inflation, but retirees who rely on savings and=
 fixed incomes are particularly sensitive to it.)</span></p><p style=3D"fon=
t:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>As older=
 people become a larger portion of the population in aging societies, their=
 political influence has also grown, and they have been getting their way. =
The result is what Vlandas calls =E2=80=9Cgerontonomia,=E2=80=9D a form of =
=E2=80=9Cdemocratically sustained economic stagnation.=E2=80=9D</span></p><=
p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px">=
<span>The disease and its symptoms are widespread. In France, youth unemplo=
yment stands at around 20 percent. GDP growth fell to zero percent in the s=
econd quarter of this year. Pension costs make up approximately a quarter o=
f all public spending, in part because the retirement age is so low, but th=
ere is little political appetite for changing it. Many, particularly on the=
 left, have argued that it is possible to preserve pensions and increase ed=
ucation spending. But the question of how to pay remains. The looming threa=
t of a </span><a href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/newsletter/KjFP7uzDIhhOTL=
53pEI_Dg~~/AAAAARA~/K3y3PtZFytXzZ2sILxvjF_QyNV7H3UEUDgiWME9jQdSzbAeJr-t9Fy3=
jHTEbFunDlVnn-KqxnFIVvimC8qQk6Hc0TGOwXKfZ0fShLKjdIbZFfmA4ZKzZfiSozbGTdUnMqg=
X-JzD4tZ-WwaVKZh8K-kCc_G8bxhQ5T9gq4Lk43d4gaecoenXQLBL9Y2PzRdYSVkh52wny7-amW=
KmAjy07H0hLLcKcYr16njorcVhj8HYFdpbeAG-_NKkP__EH80q-CFfUtJii6SUMR-rqjAAWUf3B=
TXEdDhjjXejSGLK11_yVgxUjJgMg3jzm6wIPZsQftJYvvQFXIRmgSvlq-LRThgAJMEZEUnmty9p=
cDawe23iSeNAWuJ8QV82Pdt_ih7U8" class=3D"css-sdwaa1" style=3D"color:#286ed0;=
-webkit-text-decoration:underline;text-decoration:underline">debt crisis</a=
><span> makes further borrowing difficult. And wealth taxes, while often po=
litically appealing, have not raised much money in the past.</span></p><tab=
le border=3D"0" cellpadding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0" style=3D"width:100%"><t=
